+### Gamma
+
+**Settings ▸ Playback ▸ External Video Output gamma** (just under the switch,
+`playback.transmitGamma`) says how FilmCraft's sRGB picture is encoded for the card:
+
+| Choice | What goes out |
+|---|---|
+| **sRGB to Rec.709 (BT.1886, gamma 2.4 monitor)** (default) | Each value decoded with the sRGB curve to linear light and re-encoded with gamma 1/2.4, so a BT.1886 display shows what an sRGB computer monitor shows. Black, white and the primaries are unchanged; mid grey (sRGB 128) goes out at Y' 132 instead of 126, a dark grey (sRGB 25) at 48 instead of 37. |
+| **As is (sRGB values unchanged)** | The sRGB code values as BT.709 code values. On a gamma 2.4 (BT.1886) monitor or TV the shadows look darker and the picture more contrasty than on the computer screen. |
+
+On the tested setup (DeckLink Mini Monitor HD to a Rec.709 TV), "sRGB to Rec.709" matched the
+reference picture from Avid Media Composer on the same TV and card. "As is" remains as an option.
+
+The change applies at once (the current frame is sent again), so the two can be compared live.
+Both are part of the conversion tables, so neither costs time. `decklink-test` uses the default
+too; `decklink-test --gamma asIs` sends the unchanged values.
+
 Only one program can use the card's output at a time: close Media Express, DaVinci Resolve or a
 running `filmcraft-cli decklink-test` first. If the card is missing or busy, FilmCraft logs one
 warning (`External Video Output is on but unavailable: …`) and carries on; turn the setting off
